Common mistakes include treating hardware and software as separate subjects. A program that ignores timing, memory limits or electrical limits will fail in a real device.

Other mistakes are:
mixing units, such as volts, millivolts and amperes;
forgetting ground connections and supply limits;
using a datasheet value outside its stated conditions;
copying code without understanding it;
trusting simulation before checking a simple case;
ignoring power, heat and noise in circuit design;
leaving the design project until the last weeks.

Also state assumptions and test results clearly. An examiner and a future employer both need to see how you reached an answer, not only the final number.
